
 Introduction

      This document discusses the major differences between a
      DOS NDIS device driver and a OS/2 NDIS device driver.
      This document is aimed at the developer and focues on
      areas that require special attention specific to an
      NDIS driver under DOS.  By providing a general overview
      of those differences, hopefully some of the potential
      pitfalls will be avoided.


 Related Documents

      Microsoft LAN Manager Programmer's Guide for NDIS

      Microsoft LAN Manager Network Device Driver Guide

      Microsoft Operating System/2 Device Driver Reference
      Guide

      IBM Disk Operating System Technical Reference Version
      3.3


 DOS vs. OS/2 Device Driver Models

      The DOS device driver consists of, at a minimum, two
      entry points: the Strategy procedure and the Interrupt
      procedure.  Usually, it will also include an interrupt
      service routine.  The Strategy and Interrupt procedures
      are used when DOS calls the device driver.  The
      interrupt routine is used to handle device dependent
      interrupts.  The interrupt service routine is required
      to save and restore any registers used, setup it's own
      stack and return from interrupt with an IRET
      instruction.  The DOS driver runs only in real mode.

      The OS/2 device driver contains, at a minimum, only one
      entry point: the Strategy procedure.  As in DOS, OS/2
      will usually contain an interrupt service routine as
      well.  The Strategy routine is used when OS/2 calls the
      device driver.  The interrupt service routine is called
      directly by OS/2 as a far procedure call to service
      interrupts.  Because OS/2 saves the registers and
      provides a stack, the interrupt service routine need do
      little more then process the interrupt and, when
      finished, return with a far RET instruction.  The
      device driver can run in either real or protected mode;
      OS/2 will call the device driver in the context and
      mode of the calling thread.


 Overview

      The major differences between DOS and OS/2 device
      drivers exist in the following areas:

           Device Header
           Initialization Request Packets
           API calls vs. INT 21 traps
           DevHlps vs. BIOS calls

      Each of these areas, as they relate to DOS and OS/2
      NDIS drivers, is discussed below.


 Device Header

      The DOS device header has the following format:

      Pointer to next header        DWORD
      Attribute                     WORD
      Offset to strategy procedure  WORD
      Offset to interrupt procedure WORD
      Name/unit field               8 BYTES

      The OS/2 device header has the following format:

      Pointer to next header        DWORD
      Device Attribute procedure    WORD
      Offset to Strategy            WORD
      Offset to IDC                 WORD
      Name/unit field               8 BYTES
      Reserved                      8 BYTES

      Notice the device headers are very similar.  The OS/2
      device header replaces the offset to the interrupt
      procedure with the offset to the inter-device
      communication point (IDC).  The OS/2 device header is 8
      Bytes longer than the DOS device header because of the
      Reserved field.  All other entries are the same.


 Initialization Request Packets

      The initialization packets vary slightly.  The DOS
      initialization packet has the following format:

           Request Header           13 BYTES
           Data_1                   BYTE
           Ending address of code   DWORD
           Pointer INIT arguments   DWORD
           Drive number             BYTE

      The OS/2 initialization packet follows the following
      format:

           Request Header           13 BYTES
           Data_1                   BYTE
           Pointer_1                DWORD
           Pointer to the INIT arguments DWORD
           Drive  number            BYTE

      Once again the packets are similar, but there are a few
      differences that require discussion.  Pointer_1 on
      entry is used by the OS/2 device driver as the device
      helper (DevHlp) entry point and is not used on entry by
      the DOS device driver.  For both DOS and OS/2, the
      return value of Pointer_1 is used to indicate the end
      of the resident part of the driver.  For OS/2,
      Pointer_1 is split, one word for the offset to the end
      of the code segment, one word for the offset to the end
      of the data segment.  For the DOS device driver,
      Pointer_1 is set to a codesegment:offset pointer that
      represents the last memory location used by the driver.
      Pointer_2 on entry for DOS and OS/2 points to the
      arguments on the "Device=" line in the config.sys file.
      On return Pointer_2 is used only by block devices and
      should be set to 0.


 API Calls vs. INT 21 traps


      Several API calls can be used by the device driver
      under OS/2 as well as DOS. The most common include:

           Device Open
           Device Close
           Device Read
           Device Write
           Device IOCTL
           Set Interrupt vector

      The access to the API functions is vastly different
      between the two operating systems.  OS/2 provides
      linked libraries and direct calls.  DOS interfaces to
      these functions via software interrupts (or traps).
      Open will be examined as a general example.

      For OPEN under DOS the software interrupt 21h is used
      (see the DOS technical Reference under function
      requests).

           AH    = 3Dh
           DS:DX = Pointer to the ASCII name
           AL    = Open Mode
           INT 21h

      OPEN under OS/2 is a direct API call (see the Microsoft
      Device Driver reference under DosOpen).

           push DS
           push Offset to name
           push DS
           push Offset to handle
           push DS
           push Offset to Action taken
           push 0
           push 0
           push 1 - open flag
           push C2h - open mode
           push 0
           push 0
           call DOSOPEN

      As is shown by the above example, the two requests vary
      greatly.  Care should be exercised to avoid probems.


 DevHlp VS. BIOS calls

      To assist the device driver, several device driver
      helper functions (DevHlp) exist under OS/2.  Similar
      functions exist under DOS, but are somewhat more
      involved.  Under OS/2 the common DevHlp items are:

           EOI       - End Of Interrupt.
           PhysToVirt- Convert physical address to virtual.
           VirtToPhys- Convert virtual address to physical.
           SetIRQ    - Set an interrupt vector to a specific
                          interrupt handler.
           UnSetIRQ  - Unset a specific interrupt vector.
           AllocPhys - Allocate physical kernel memory
           FreePhys  - Free memory allocated by AllocPhys
           SetTimer  - Setup a timer tick handler.
           ResetTimer- Unset a timer tick handler.

      To perform an EOI under DOS, you send a non-specific
      end of interrupt to I/O address 21.  To perform an EOI
      under OS/2, you make a DevHlp EOI call (DevHlp_EOI)
      that implements a specific EOI.

      PhysToVirt and VirtToPhys unfortunately are not
      provided under DOS.  However, it is quite simple to
      write routines to replace them.

      SetIRQ and UnsetIRQ are accomplished by a function
      request using software interrupt 21h and a function
      request code of 35h.  However, these trap functions do
      not unmask the interrupt at the interrupt controller,
      whereas, the equivalent OS/2 DevHlp function does.

      AllocPhys under OS/2 does not have an equivalent
      function trap under DOS.  Each driver must manage this
      on a case by case basis.

      To setup a timer interrupt handler under DOS, you hook
      the timer interrupt vector using the software interrupt
      21h while also saving the old interrupt vector.  To
      release the timer, use the software interrupt 21h and
      replace the hooked vector with the old vector.

